## 6. Code Review Checklist

- [ ] Only entry points (controllers, triggers, batch, queueables) call `createNewUOW()`
- [ ] Only entry points call `commitWork()`, once per transaction
- [ ] Service/helper methods never call `createNewUOW()` or `commitWork()`
- [ ] UOW dependencies in helpers are explicitly passed
- [ ] No selectors called inside loops
- [ ] Selector methods return all needed fields
- [ ] Domain classes encapsulate business logic, not data access or DML
- [ ] Feature flags and record type checks live at top of methods
- [ ] Logging uses structured logging instead of `System.debug`

---

## 7. Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Multiple Commits in Same Method
Triggers fire twice, governor limits consumed twice, risk of partial success.

### ❌ Service Method Committing
Service is no longer composable, can't be called from other services safely.

### ❌ Hidden UOW Dependency
Not clear from signature that UOW must exist, hard to test, easy to break.

### ❌ SOQL in Loop
Governor limit violations, slow performance.

### ❌ No Empty Check Before .get(0)
`List index out of bounds` exception if no records returned.

---

## 8. Quick Reference

| Scenario | Pattern |
|----------|---------|
| Controller entry point | Create UOW → call services → commit once |
| Service method | Get current UOW → register work → DON'T commit |
| Domain method | Modify in-memory records → DON'T commit |
| Helper method | Receive UOW as parameter → register work |
| Multiple updates to same record | Use Map to consolidate → register once |
| Selector results | Check `isEmpty()` before `.get(0)` |
| Feature flags | Check at method start with early return |
| Error handling | Use structured logging, not `System.debug` |

